  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\n===== Today through Friday =====\n\nAnother chilly start with early morning temps in the 30s, though \nGOES Nighttime Fog channel shows less valley fog so far compared to \nyesterday. May still see some patchy fog through the morning as we \ncontinue toward the diurnal minimum just before sunrise. Expect \nanother sunny day with strong sfc high pressure centered over WV/PA \ntoday. Temps are forecast to reach the low to mid 60s with light \nsoutheast winds. \n\nDry and tranquil weather will continue through Thursday and Friday \ntoo as the sfc high remains stalled over the mid-Atlantic region. \nHowever, we'll begin to tap into deeper SW flow, leading to a slight \nincrease in WAA. Temps will nudge a few degrees warmer for the end \nof the week, with highs likely in the upper 60s and close to 70. \nFriday night looks to be a little more mild with lows in the 40s \ninstead of the 30s. \n\n===== Weekend and into Next Week =====\n\nAs we head into the weekend, a large upper trough will be noted over \nthe western US. This will promote deeper SW flow across the Ohio \nValley for the weekend, which should translate to highs on Saturday \nreaching the upper 60s and near 70. As this trough pivots across the \ncountry, it will drive a weak cold front through the region by \nSunday night. Ahead of the front, our next rain chances arrive \nsometime earlier on Sunday. The global solutions have backed off on \nprecip amounts and forcing with this wave since yesterday, and now \nsuggest weaker mid-level vorticity and the LLJ remaining more to the \nwest, limiting our moisture fetch. We'll take any rain we can get, \nbut overall guidance is not overly impressive. NBM 24-hour QPF \nprobabilities show a 30% chance of more than 0.10\" and just a 15% \nchance of greater than 0.25\". Light rain will exit west to east \nSunday night or Monday morning as the front slides through the \nforecast area.\n\nBehind the cold front, much cooler air will begin to filter into our \narea by Sunday night and Monday morning. Temps will fall into the \n30s for most by Monday morning. However, highs on Monday are \nforecast to only reach the low 50s. Cool weather continues for next \nweek, with overnight temps below freezing and possible highs on \nTuesday and Wednesday in the upper 40s and low 50s.",
